<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Petronilla of Aragon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1136–<a href="/years/1173">1173</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Petronila of Aragon · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jimenez-dynasty">Jiménez dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Petronilla was queen of Aragon from 1137 to 1164, the daughter and heiress of Ramiro II. Betrothed as an infant in 1137 to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramon-berenguer-iv-count-of-barcelona">Ramon Berenguer IV, Count of Barcelona</a>, who then governed Aragon, she married him in 1150. The union joined Aragon and the County of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> in what became the Crown of Aragon, and in 1164 she abdicated in favour of their son Alfonso II.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/petronilla-of-aragon.jpg" alt="Petronilla of Aragon" width="320" height="380" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Escarlati; Genealogía anónima por iniciativa de Martín I de Aragón, &quot;El Humano · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Peronella_reina.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Petronilla was the daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramiro-ii-of-aragon">Ramiro II of Aragon</a> and Agnes of Aquitaine. Her father was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Barbastro-Roda when his brother Alfonso I died childless in 1134. Alfonso had left the kingdom to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-templar">Knights Templar</a>, the Hospitallers and the Knights of the Holy Sepulchre, but his decision was not respected: the aristocracy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a> elected a king of their own, restoring their independence, and the Aragonese nobility raised Ramiro to the throne. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-innocent-ii">Pope Innocent II</a> rejected the election, and Ramiro married Agnes in 1135 without papal approval.</p><p>Petronilla's marriage was a very important matter of state. The nobility rejected a proposal by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-vii-of-leon-and-castile">Alfonso VII of Castile</a> to marry her to his son Sancho and have her educated at his court. Instead, when she was a little over a year old, she was betrothed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barbastro">Barbastro</a> on 11 August 1137 to Raymond Berengar IV, Count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a>, who was twenty-three years her senior.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>At El Castellar on 13 November 1137 Ramiro abdicated, transferred authority to Ramon Berenguer and returned to monastic life. Ramon Berenguer ruled the kingdom under the title Prince of the Aragonese. The betrothal was ratified at a wedding in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lleida">Lleida</a> in August 1150, when Petronilla was fourteen, and the marriage was consummated early in 1151. It produced five children, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-ii-of-aragon">Alfonso II of Aragon</a>, born in 1157, and Sancho, born in 1161.</p><p>In April 1152, pregnant with her first child, she wrote a will leaving her kingdom to her husband should she die in childbirth, and in it she already styled herself Queen of Aragon. While her husband was in Provence in 1156–57, she presided over the court from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a>. After his death in 1162 she received the County of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/besalu">Besalú</a> and the Vall de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ribe">Ribes</a> for life and was the only ruler of Aragon for the next two years.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>On 18 June 1164 Petronilla abdicated in favour of her seven-year-old son, who took the name Alfonso out of deference to the Aragonese. The historian Jerónimo Zurita described her as regent during his minority. She died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> in October 1173 and was buried in Barcelona Cathedral; her tomb has been lost.</p><p>Historians have long debated whether she was the true ruler of Aragon, since some hold that Ramiro gave the kingdom to his son-in-law and that her role was secondary. Zurita reported a clause in the pact with Ramon Berenguer that Aragon would pass to the children of a later marriage if she died. Her case served as a precedent after the death of King Martin, when Ferdinand I was enthroned under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/compromise-of-caspe">Compromise of Caspe</a>, his claim resting on descent through a woman.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Petronilla of Aragon” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His daughter <b>Petronila</b> was betrothed in 1137, when still an infant, to Ramon Berenguer IV, Count of Barcelona, who governed Aragon as its prince while keeping his own counties.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crown-of-aragon">Crown of Aragon · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Ramiro II, a monk called from the cloister to the throne in 1134, married to provide an heir and retired to monastic life once his baby daughter <b>Petronila</b> was betrothed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-aragon">Kingdom of Aragon · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>His marriage to <b>Petronila</b>, heiress of the Kingdom of Aragon, agreed in 1137, joined the two dynasties, and in 1162 their son Alfonso II inherited Barcelona and soon Aragon, so that the Catalan counties became one constituent of the Crown of Aragon.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-catalonia">Principality of Catalonia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1137 Ramon Berenguer IV was betrothed to <b>Petronilla</b>, heiress of Aragon, and governed both lands.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-barcelona">County of Barcelona</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huesca">Huesca</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=42.14,-0.41&amp;year=1160" aria-label="Huesca on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4540 664 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Petronilla of Aragon"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4990 934h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4990 934h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=42.14,-0.41&amp;year=1160">What was here in 1160? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
